> Thanks for pointing this out! I dropped the second residual correlation and now the model converge with no problem using the subsamples.

Your construct still is not identified unless you drop all residual correlations, or impose equality constraints on the factor loadings.  Count your parameters and sample moments.

> But then I don't understand why was it able to converge using the full sample? I always thought that if the model can converge then it means there is no identification problem.

Not necessarily.  Identification rules are largely heuristic and difficult to program checks for (see some recent heated discussion on this topic in the April SEMNET  <http://www2.gsu.edu/~mkteer/semnet.html>archives, centered around Judea Pearl challenging conventional wisdom).  Sometimes an optimization algorithm can find a local maximum and stop searching, thinking it found a unique solution.

